Testing a policy whose 'chain' names more than one chain.

'chain' takes a space-separated list (or a UCI list), so one policy can route
both forwarded traffic (prerouting) and the router's own (output). Each chain
gets the same rules, and a domain policy still builds ONE set and ONE dnsmasq
nftset entry per domain, both chains matching that set -- which is the point:
two policies with the same domain list need two sets that dnsmasq fills on
every answer, and two lists that have to be kept in step by hand.

A chain pbr does not create would emit a rule nft cannot place, rejecting the
whole ruleset; such a policy is now rejected on its own. A Tor policy is the
exception: its rules always land in dstnat, so 'chain' only ever earned it a
warning, and it still does -- with the rules emitted once, not once per chain.

